Third-party and derived material
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

crates/dw4core/data/*.json

    The Digimon World 4 item database: item names, ids, categories, grades and
    mod-chip family layouts. Vendored verbatim from the reverse-engineering
    work used by the original Python editor. The names are the game's own
    strings, extracted from AREA.AFS and the game executable's string table.

crates/dw4core/tests/fixtures/

    Golden test fixtures. fixtures/mcd001/save.raw is an 81,920-byte save
    extracted from a real PlayStation 2 memory-card image, and
    mcd001/expected.json is what the Python editor reads from it.
    fixtures/builder/*.raw are saves synthesised by the Python editor's
    dw4build.py.

External reverse-engineering references (not required for building)

    The reverse-engineering artefacts these tables were derived from: Ghidra
    findings, decoded Lua, save dumps and format documentation for
    Digimon World 4 (USA, SLUS_208.36).

Digimon World 4 and all related names are trademarks of their respective
owners. This project is an unaffiliated fan tool and ships no game code.

Regional PS2 support (0.2.0) was additionally verified against the European
SLES_534.05 and Japanese SLPS_254.53 releases. The vendored catalogue and original
format fixtures retain their USA provenance; regional support does not imply
localized catalogue labels or complete campaign testing in every release.

GameCube filesystem references (0.3.0)

    The raw memory-card layout, checksum algorithm, and mirrored directory/BAT
    selection rules were checked against Dolphin Emulator's public
    Source/Core/Core/HW/GCMemcard/GCMemcard.h and GCMemcard.cpp.
    Dolphin Emulator Project, GPL-2.0-or-later:
    https://github.com/dolphin-emu/dolphin

    GameCube editing was verified against the USA GDJEB2 retail release using
    privately held GCI and raw-card samples generated in Dolphin. The samples,
    game banners and private emulator control patch are not shipped here.

Xbox filesystem and signing references (0.4.0)
--------------------------------------------
The native FATX reader/writer was implemented with reference to xemu and
mborgerson/fatx (https://github.com/mborgerson/fatx), and checked against files
extracted by pyfatx. The title-key derivation was independently checked against
PMStanley/xbox-save-sig (https://github.com/PMStanley/xbox-save-sig), live kernel
memory and retail signatures. No game executable, save fixture or console
firmware is included with the Xbox implementation.
